+# Native TypeScript checking with tsrs
+
+The Node executor build uses `tsrs@0.18.0-ts7.1.0-dev.20260929` for its no-emit
+typecheck. esbuild still produces both runtime bundles. Its TypeScript
+configuration uses bundler module resolution to match that build instead of the
+removed Node 10 resolver. Cargo watches this configuration as well as the package
+manifest and sources before invoking the filtered Turbo build.
+
+The CLI also accepts `"typescriptCompiler": "tsrs"` in `convex.json` and
+`convex typecheck --typescript-compiler tsrs`. Projects must install tsrs locally;
+selection never silently switches to another compiler. The existing enable,
+try and disable modes retain their missing-compiler behavior. The standalone
+SDK carries the same selection support.
+
+This is a build-tool patch with no backend wire-protocol dependency. Older
+convex-js clients that use tsc remain compatible: the backend receives the same
+JavaScript and deployment requests, and does not require clients to select or
+install tsrs. The CLI default remains tsc; tsrs is an explicit project choice. tsrs 0.18
+supports Linux x64/arm64 and macOS arm64. It implements TypeScript 7.1 development
+semantics, not the TypeScript 5 baseline used by several other packages. Keep
+their tsc invocations until their configuration, declarations and
+standard-library contracts have been migrated deliberately. In particular,
+system UDFs still emit ES5 and the SDK still emits declarations against its
+existing baseline; the UDF runtime implements a deliberately limited browser API
+surface.
+
+Reverting this patch restores the Node executor's tsc check and removes the
+additional CLI selector. It does not require a running service restart.
